From 60a3a1827e42cebb9f087b47589e4f26ec136551 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: test <test@local>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 00:50:36 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] P09.x (1.13.5): class-body method-modifier keywords +
 verify-override
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add C++-style method-modifier keywords inside a `class` body:

    (pub)? (virtual | override | constructor)* fn …

These are pure parser sugar over the existing attributes, so all
downstream layout/codegen is unchanged:

  * `constructor`  ->  #[constructor]
  * `virtual`      ->  #[cpp_virtual]
  * `override`     ->  #[cpp_virtual] + #[rustc_cxx_override]

`virtual` and `override` are reserved Rust keywords; `constructor` is
a *contextual* keyword (only a modifier when a method follows, so a
field literally named `constructor` still parses as a field).
Combining a keyword modifier with an explicit `#[…]` attribute on the
same method is a hard error (use one form or the other).

`#[rustc_cxx_override]` is a new no-args internal marker attribute. It
has no codegen effect; `check_attr` uses it to verify the method
actually overrides a base-class virtual (walking the polymorphic base
chain by method name, mirroring `virtuals_on_chain`'s by-name slot
replacement). If it does not, that is a hard error — catching the
footgun where a misspelled override silently appends a *new* vtable
slot instead of replacing the base's.

Touch points for the new attribute follow the existing
`#[cpp_virtual]` / `#[rustc_cxx_base]` wiring: symbol, AttributeKind
variant, cross-crate encoding (Yes), no-args parser + registration,
builtin_attrs entry, and the check_attr dispatch arm.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
